//
// Phase J7a operator gate: norm_tail.
//
// The norm tail -- add, LayerNorm, gamma scale -- as three herds in one
// segment joined by L1->L1 channels, with x and residual packed into one L3
// buffer so the segment holds two shim MM2S streams per column and never
// enters the packet path. Same function as addnorm's pre-add form, checked
// against the same FP32 oracle; what this gate adds is the dataflow: the sum
// and the normalized tensor stay resident in L1 instead of round-tripping
// through L3 the way pattern/fused's decomposed tail does.
//
// Three arms, because the phase's claim has three parts that no single check
// can see at once:
//
// 1. STRUCTURE, first because it is host-only and fails fast: both claimed
//    shapes lowered through the aircc pipeline prefix ending at
//    air-dma-to-channel must carry ZERO packet-typed channels. A third
//    L3-facing stream would put every input channel on the packet queue --
//    correct at one trip, silently wrong past it -- while the numerical arm
//    still passed.
// 2. NUMBERS, on real NPU2: opcheck.py against an FP32 reference, np.isclose
//    over the full output at rtol=1.6e-2, zero permitted mismatches, both
//    shapes -- 128x768 (two trips per tile) and the block's own 4096x768
//    (128 trips, where the precision claim is measured). The spec rows also
//    carry mean_rel_L1_max 1.688e-2, the whole-layer figure the resident
//    pipeline must beat, enforced by the same run (measured 4.354e-3 /
//    4.478e-3 -- see opcheck_specs.py). The inputs are drawn asymmetrically
//    (x and residual from different distributions) so a plane-addressing bug
//    in the packed fetch cannot hide behind LayerNorm's scale invariance.
// 3. The NEGATIVE CONTROL, on the cheap two-trip shape: one element of the
//    packed device buffer perturbed after the reference is computed, and the
//    comparison itself must reject the run. Its verdict comes from
//    opcheck.py's own inversion, which only reports the control satisfied
//    when the completed comparison did the rejecting -- a missing toolchain
//    or an absent NPU exits non-zero without comparing anything.
//
// NPU serialization lives one layer down, not in the recipe below. Device
// access is locked on /tmp/npu.lock, which XRTRunner.run_test holds across
// xclbin load and dispatch -- the same inode lit.cfg.py's NPU substitution
// and KernelCache take, so this interlocks with every other NPU job in the
// repository. Taking the outer /tmp/mlir-air-npu.lock here instead would
// DEADLOCK against the gate command that invokes this suite.
//
// Editing this header: lit scans the WHOLE file for its directives, so naming
// one in prose -- even inside a comment -- registers as a second directive and
// leaves the test UNRESOLVED. Describe them, never spell them out.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2, peano
//
// RUN: mkdir -p test_npu2_norm_tail_peano
// RUN: cd test_npu2_norm_tail_peano
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile clean
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile check-norm-tail-structure | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=STRUCT
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile check-norm-tail PEANO_INSTALL_DIR=%PEANO_INSTALL_DIR | FileCheck %s
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile check-norm-tail-fault PEANO_INSTALL_DIR=%PEANO_INSTALL_DIR | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FAULT
//
// The packet count is stated inside the per-shape verdict line, so a lowering
// that found packet channels cannot match here -- and a lowering that never
// ran and counted a vacuous zero is failed by the script itself, which also
// requires no unlowered DMAs and both named L1-to-L1 channels present.
// STRUCT: [norm-tail-structure] norm_tail [128x768]: PASS (0 packet-typed
// STRUCT: [norm-tail-structure] norm_tail [4096x768]: PASS (0 packet-typed
// STRUCT: [norm-tail-structure] PASS
//
// Both lines per shape, not just the verdict: the first is XRTRunner's own,
// the second names the operator and shape, so a test wired to the wrong
// operator fails here rather than passing quietly.
// CHECK: PASS!
// CHECK: [opcheck] norm_tail [128x768]: PASS
// CHECK: PASS!
// CHECK: [opcheck] norm_tail [4096x768]: PASS
//
// The injection line first: it names the element and both values, so an
// injection that silently perturbed nothing is visible here rather than being
// inferred from the verdict. (127, 0, 0) is the last row's x plane in the
// [rows, 2, cols] packing -- see prepare_norm_tail on why the x half.
// FAULT: [fault-inject] input 0 at (127, 0, 0)
// FAULT: [opcheck] norm_tail [128x768]: FAIL
// FAULT: NEGATIVE CONTROL: PASS
